---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About NO BALL GAMES | From Restricted Spaces to Street Culture Brand"
  description="Learn the story behind NO BALL GAMES, a raw street-culture brand turning public restriction, graffiti energy and estate identity into a creative movement."
  current="about"
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <span class="eyebrow">About the brand</span>
    <h1>From a warning sign to a creative weapon.</h1>
    <p>
      NO BALL GAMES starts with a simple British street image: a sign telling people what they cannot do. The brand flips that message. It takes the restriction, the estate memory, the wall texture and the attitude, then turns it into identity.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section split-grid">
    <article class="panel-card">
      <span class="kicker">The meaning</span>
      <h2>Denied space becomes owned space.</h2>
      <p>
        The sign is supposed to shut down play. In this brand, it becomes the opposite: a reminder to create even when the space was never designed for you.
      </p>
      <p>
        NO BALL GAMES stands for people who grew up around hard concrete, rules, warnings, locked courts, blank walls and public spaces that felt controlled by everyone except the people living there.
      </p>
      <a class="inline-link" href={`${base}lore/`}>Read the lore archive →</a>
    </article>

    <article class="panel-card">
      <span class="kicker">The energy</span>
      <ul class="list-clean">
        <li>Graffiti attitude without fake polish.</li>
        <li>British estate culture and urban memory.</li>
        <li>Grime art, streetwear, characters and collectibles.</li>
        <li>Digital-first culture without losing the real-world edge.</li>
        <li>A brand system that can expand into art, games, clothing and lore.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2>Origin timeline</h2>
      <p>How the sign became a brand system.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="timeline">
      <article class="timeline-item">
        <strong>Start</strong>
        <div>
          <h3>The sign exists everywhere</h3>
          <p>Yellow enamel plates, painted concrete warnings, stencilled fences. The phrase is part of British urban landscape — instantly recognisable, rarely questioned.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="timeline-item">
        <strong>Flip</strong>
        <div>
          <h3>Restriction becomes identity</h3>
          <p>The decision: take the warning, own the phrase, build a brand around the feeling of being told where not to be. The ban is the badge.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="timeline-item">
        <strong>Build</strong>
        <div>
          <h3>Art, clothing and characters emerge</h3>
          <p>Original paintings, hand-finished jackets, character designs, street typography and visual experiments start developing the brand vocabulary.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="timeline-item">
        <strong>Expand</strong>
        <div>
          <h3>Lore, collectibles and community</h3>
          <p>The brand extends into digital collectibles, a story universe, collector drops, gaming concepts and a community layer built around shared cultural values. <a class="inline-link" href={`${base}lore/`}>See the lore →</a></p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2>Brand rules</h2>
      <p>The site should feel clean enough to trust and raw enough to believe.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <article class="feature-card">
        <span class="kicker">Rule one</span>
        <h3>No fake corporate voice</h3>
        <p>Keep the language direct, visual and culturally grounded. The brand should sound like it came from the wall, not a boardroom.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature-card">
        <span class="kicker">Rule two</span>
        <h3>Real culture first</h3>
        <p>Art, people, places, stories and community matter more than empty hype. Digital tools should support the culture, not replace it.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature-card">
        <span class="kicker">Rule three</span>
        <h3>Build with depth</h3>
        <p>Every page should add something: story, proof, context, product value, community value or collector value.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section split-grid">
    <article class="panel-card">
      <span class="kicker">Explore further</span>
      <h2>Go deeper into the world.</h2>
      <p>The brand has multiple layers. Start with the projects, dig into the lore, browse the collections or connect with the community.</p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="button primary" href={`${base}projects/`}>View projects</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href={`${base}community/`}>Join community</a>
      </div>
    </article>
    <article class="panel-card">
      <span class="kicker">Quick links</span>
      <ul class="list-clean">
        <li><a class="inline-link" href={`${base}lore/`}>Lore archive — characters, signs and restricted zones</a></li>
        <li><a class="inline-link" href={`${base}collections/`}>Collections — physical art, clothing and digital drops</a></li>
        <li><a class="inline-link" href={`${base}research/`}>Research vault — brand notes and source material</a></li>
        <li><a class="inline-link" href={`${base}contact/`}>Contact — collaborations and enquiries</a></li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
